; empty_loop_skip_scan (QUI-1056): pins the step cost of skipping an empty
; loop body.
;
; A RANGE with count = 0 does not enter its body. Both implementations
; instead scan forward through the instruction stream, tracking nesting
; depth, until they reach the matching NEXT -- work that is O(body length)
; and that spec/xqvm/METERING.md charges BASE_STEPS per instruction
; consumed. The body here nests a second (never-executed) RANGE/NEXT pair
; so the scan's depth tracking is exercised too.
;
; Steps:
;   dispatched: PUSH 0, PUSH 0, RANGE, PUSH 7, HALT       = 5 * BASE_STEPS
;   skipped:    NOP, PUSH 1, PUSH 1, RANGE, NOP, NEXT,
;               NOP, NEXT                                 = 8 * BASE_STEPS
;   total:                                                  13
;
; The eight skipped instructions are metered but never dispatched, so they
; do not appear in the instruction count. The final stack holds only the
; sentinel 7: had the body run, the nested RANGE's two operands would have
; been consumed and its loop frame entered instead.

PUSH 0         ; start
PUSH 0         ; count = 0 -- the loop body is skipped
RANGE

NOP
PUSH 1
PUSH 1
RANGE          ; nested opener the scan must count as depth+1
NOP
NEXT           ; closes the nested opener, not the outer one
NOP
NEXT           ; the matching NEXT the scan stops at

PUSH 7
HALT
